cathay pacific only recently began  recovering from protests in hong kong  that badly hit demand last year  but the pandemics dealt a second blow  with international borders closed  the hong kong-based airline saw its  passengers dropped by nearly 82 percent  in the first eight months of this year  it's announced it will cut nearly a  quarter of its staff  and shut down its regional unit cafe  dragon  analysts say they're not surprised  unfortunately  it has to be done and now they can focus  on some other areas of restructuring  and making the company more efficient  for the near term because it's going to  take a long time before the market  recovers  cafe pacific is not the only airline in  the region to undergo a major  restructuring last month  singapore airlines announced the largest  retrenchment in its history  cutting more than 20 of its staff thai  airways filed for bankruptcy protection  in may  here in malaysia the national carrier is  in talks to restructure its debts  but the finance minister recently said  the government will not provide any  financial relief or debt guarantees  raising questions about the fate and  future of malaysia airlines  more gloomy days are predicted to lie  ahead the international air transport  association  forecasts passenger traffic will not  recover until  2024 and now a tech startup has begun  testing  a digital solution this is a a sort of a  digital  travel health certificate that a lot  that people are  looking to use to open up borders  instead of using quarantines  so if the industry could get behind  something like that where  governments around the world would  accept that then airlines like cafe  wouldn't be in such  trouble it's called the common pass and  aims to be the first  globally recognized document that a  passenger has tested negative for covet  19 before a flight  the airline industry is hoping this will  help encourage  air travel even if it's only by a small  step  florence louis al jazeera kuala lumpur
 

